<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/11th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chanyuan Treaty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1005">1005</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chanyuan Treaty was a peace agreement concluded in 1005 between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a> and the Khitan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a>. The Song agreed to pay annual tribute in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> in return for peace along the northern frontier. It formalised a tributary relationship that brought long-lasting peace but left the northern territories in Liao hands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chanyuan-treaty.jpg" alt="Chanyuan Treaty" width="280" height="307" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 玖巧仔 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liao_and_Song_Dynasties.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Relations between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, founded in 960, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> were at first cordial while the Song conquered the southern states. In 979 the Song destroyed Northern Han, a Shatuo Turk state under Liao protection, but what concerned them more was Liao's hold on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-prefectures">Sixteen Prefectures</a>, which included modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>. Emperor Taizong's march on them ended in rout at the Battle of Gaoliang River, and a second attempt in three columns in 986 was defeated on every front. From 999 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shengzong-of-liao">Emperor Shengzong of Liao</a> attacked the Song every year.</p><p>In 1004 Shengzong led a campaign in person and camped about 160 kilometres north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> capital, Bianjing. Emperor Zhenzong reluctantly marched north to Chan Prefecture, the first large town beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>, whose defences had been prepared by the planner Kou Zhun. The emperor's arrival lifted Song morale, and late in 1004 a Song crossbow killed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> general Xiao Talin, crippling the Khitan offensive. Wang Jizhong, a Song officer captured in 1003 and given rank by the Liao, urged peace on Empress Dowager Xiao, who held power at the Liao court.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>A ceasefire was reached in January 1005, and the envoy Cao Liyong concluded the agreement with Empress Dowager Xiao. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> were to send 200,000 bolts of raw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and 100,000 taels of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> every year. Existing moats and city walls could be repaired, but no new ones built. The two states became sibling nations: Shengzong called Zhenzong his elder brother, and Zhenzong called the Empress Dowager his aunt. Wang Jizhong had persuaded her to give up territorial claims.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The treaty brought about a century of relative peace, which lasted until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> and the Jurchen Jin agreed in the Alliance Conducted at Sea to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> together. Liao used the payments to build its central capital, Zhongjing, and a wide trade network, while the Song kept disputed territory for a burden far smaller than its military spending, though the treaty also admitted the loss of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-prefectures">Sixteen Prefectures</a>. Under pressure from Liao and Western Xia, the Song raised the payments in 1042 to 300,000 bolts and 200,000 taels. The framework shaped Song relations with Western Xia and Jin and was copied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>, and for the first time two Sons of Heaven recognised each other.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chanyuan Treaty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1004 the Empress Dowager Chengtian and Emperor Shengzong led an army deep into Song territory to <b>Chanyuan</b>, near the Yellow River, forcing the treaty that brought more than a century of peace.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>It never recovered the Sixteen Prefectures around modern Beijing, which the Khitan Liao dynasty held, and under the <b>Chanyuan Treaty</b> of 1005 it agreed to costly annual payments of silk and silver to the Liao.</blockquote><a href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
